-- =============================================================================
-- 0052_sandhyavandanam_self_contained.sql
--
-- GENERATED by scripts/build-sandhya-selfcontained.mjs. Do not hand-edit.
--
-- Two faults, both of which make the reader do work the app exists to do.
--
-- STEPS THAT RECITE ALL THREE SITTINGS AT ONCE. Arghya Pradanam said "THREE
-- times in the morning and in the evening; TWICE at noon"; Gayatri Japa said
-- "MORNING 108 ... NOON 32 ... EVENING 64". The reader has already said which
-- sitting they are keeping and is then handed all three to pick from -- at the
-- one moment in the rite when they are counting on their fingers rather than
-- reading. Each becomes three mode-tagged steps.
--
-- Not a new convention: Sankalpam, Apaam Prashanam, Japa Sankalpam, Gayatri
-- Upasthanam and the Surya/Varuna Prarthana already split exactly this way.
-- These two were left as prose because what differs is the COUNT and not the
-- words -- which is precisely why prose fails here. A count buried in a
-- sentence is easier to misread than a mantra that is plainly not yours.
--
-- STEPS THAT NAME ANOTHER STEP INSTEAD OF BEING ONE. Japasthanam Prokshanam
-- opened "Do Achamanam and Anga Vandanam once", and the Navagraha tarpanam
-- closed the same way. Someone who knows the rite reads that as an
-- instruction; someone learning it has to leave the step, find step one, and
-- come back. The book says only "(do aachamanam & angavandanam once)" because
-- it is a reference card for someone who already knows. This app is not.
--
-- So the pair is inserted as real steps at both places the book asks for them.
-- Also not new: Vighneshwara Dhyanam and Pranayamam are already repeated in
-- full as "(japa)" variants rather than back-referenced.
--
-- THE CLONES ARE INSERT...SELECT. Each repeated step copies its mantra and
-- meaning from the row it repeats rather than retyping Devanagari to say the
-- same thing twice, which is how an error gets into one of two places and then
-- has to be found by eye.
--
-- 53 steps in all, 39 in any one sitting (was 45 and 35).
-- =============================================================================

begin;

-- Park every step clear of the numbers about to be assigned. Renumbering in
-- place would collide the moment a step moves onto a number still occupied,
-- and the order of the updates would silently decide the outcome.
update public.pooja_steps set step_number = step_number + 1000 where pooja_id = 'sandhyavandanam';

